  {
    "objectID": "setup.html",
    "href": "setup.html",
    "title": "Setup",
    "section": "",
    "text": "1. Python\nBefore session 2, install two things: Python and the VS Code editor. Then run a two-line script to check that everything works.\nWe use Python 3.14 from python.org. It comes with IDLE, the simple editor we open in session 2.",
    "crumbs": [
      "Setup"
    ]
  },
  {
    "objectID": "setup.html#python",
    "href": "setup.html#python",
    "title": "Setup",
    "section": "",
    "text": "WindowsmacOS\n\n\n\nDownload the Python install manager from python.org/downloads/windows and install it.\nOpen Terminal / PowerShell or Command Prompt from the Start menu and type:\npy install 3.14\nIf it asks whether to add a folder to PATH or change other settings, accept.\nCheck the installation:\npy --version\nIt should print Python 3.14. followed by a number.\n\nIDLE is now in the Start menu under Python 3.14 → IDLE (Python 3.14).\nIf the install manager gives you trouble, the same page still offers the classic Windows installer (64-bit) for Python 3.14. On its first screen, tick Add python.exe to PATH before clicking Install Now.\n\n\n\nDownload the macOS installer for the latest Python 3.14 from python.org/downloads/macos and run it, accepting the defaults.\nAt the end, a Finder window opens the folder Applications → Python 3.14. Double-click Install Certificates.command there; it lets Python download files from the internet.\nOpen Terminal (in Applications → Utilities) and check the installation:\npython3 --version\nIt should print Python 3.14. followed by a number.\n\nIDLE is in Applications → Python 3.14 → IDLE.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNoteAlready using conda?\n\n\n\nIf you already work with Anaconda, Miniconda or Miniforge, you can keep it: create an environment with conda create -n ars-pythonica python=3.14 and select it in VS Code (see section 3). The instructions in this book assume the python.org installation, so details may differ for you.",
    "crumbs": [
      "Setup"
    ]
  },
  {
    "objectID": "setup.html#vs-code",
    "href": "setup.html#vs-code",
    "title": "Setup",
    "section": "2. VS Code",
    "text": "2. VS Code\nVS Code is the editor we use from session 2 on. It needs the Python extension to run Python code.\n\nWindowsmacOS\n\n\n\nDownload the Windows User Installer from code.visualstudio.com/download and run it. Keep Add to PATH ticked.\nOpen VS Code, press Ctrl+Shift+X (Extensions), search for Python, and install the extension by Microsoft.\nSearch for Jupyter and install the extension by Microsoft, so that VS Code can open Jupyter notebooks (.ipynb files).\n\n\n\n\nDownload the Mac version from code.visualstudio.com/download, open the download, and move Visual Studio Code into Applications.\nOpen VS Code, press Cmd+Shift+X (Extensions), search for Python, and install the extension by Microsoft.\nSearch for Jupyter and install the extension by Microsoft, so that VS Code can open Jupyter notebooks (.ipynb files).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNoteDon’t forget about ipykernel\n\n\n\nWhen you create or open an .ipynb notebook for the first time and run any cell, VS Code may prompt you to install ipykernel - this package is necessary to execute Jupyter notebook code. Accept and install.",
    "crumbs": [
      "Setup"
    ]
  },
  {
    "objectID": "setup.html#did-it-work",
    "href": "setup.html#did-it-work",
    "title": "Setup",
    "section": "3. Did it work?",
    "text": "3. Did it work?\n\nCreate a local folder for the course, e.g. ars-pythonica, and open it in VS Code (File → Open Folder).\nCreate a new file hello.py in it and type or paste:\ngreeting = 'χαῖρε, κόσμε'\nprint(greeting)\nSave the file and click the ▷ button (Run Python File) in the top right corner. If VS Code asks for an interpreter, choose Python 3.14.\n\n\nWindowsmacOS\n\n\nThe terminal at the bottom should show:\nχαῖρε, κόσμε\nIf VS Code can’t find Python, press Ctrl+Shift+P, run Python: Select Interpreter, and choose Python 3.14.\n\n\nThe terminal at the bottom should show:\nχαῖρε, κόσμε\nIf VS Code can’t find Python, press Cmd+Shift+P, run Python: Select Interpreter, and choose Python 3.14.\n\n\n\nCheck the accents and breathings in both the editor and the terminal. If something fails, save the error message (copied as text, or a screenshot) and bring it along so that we can discuss it.",
    "crumbs": [
      "Setup"
    ]
  },
